sections.body:
The **cost-benefit analysis (CBA)** tests whether the project makes economic sense for society. It should already exist from the Identification Phase, but it was built on **preliminary data**. During appraisal the project becomes much more detailed, and the Guide says this new information **must** be put into the CBA and its results revisited. At a minimum:
- the **detailed project scope** (infrastructure design and services), which helps identify users and other stakeholders whose costs and benefits count;
- **willingness-to-pay** evaluations from the demand study, which sharpen the estimate of economic benefits;
- the **technical specifications**, which give a much more precise estimate of **whole-life costs**;
- the **risk assessments**, which adjust the economic data.

The refreshed CBA is an essential part of the final appraisal report and an important driver of the **green light decision** (the formal decision to proceed to structuring and tender). Some countries only complete a full CBA at this stage; they simply run the Chapter 3 method with the better appraisal data.

**Climate change.** Early assessment of climate risks is critical because they can change economic feasibility. Expected economic losses from climate impacts **must** be quantified over the life cycle, using different climate scenarios, so the project stays feasible even if it suffers climate impacts. These climate parameters can then be used in the CBA or the value for money analysis.
